{"id":"synthetic-antenna-2024","title":"Adaptive nulling in small antenna arrays for interference-limited links","year":2024,"scholarName":"Test Scholar","audience":"general","origin":"harvested","synthetic":true,"text":"Abstract. We study whether a four-element adaptive array can suppress co-channel interference on a 2.4 GHz link without prior knowledge of interferer direction. Using a least-mean-squares (LMS) weight update, the array steers pattern nulls toward interferers while holding gain on the desired signal.\n\nMethod. Four patch elements were spaced at half a wavelength. Weights were updated every 2 ms using the LMS rule with a step size of 0.01. We ran 120 trials across three interference geometries: one interferer at 30 degrees, two interferers at 30 and 110 degrees, and four interferers spread between 20 and 160 degrees. The desired signal arrived at broadside in every trial.\n\nResults. With one interferer the array improved the signal-to-interference ratio by a median of 14.2 dB. With two interferers the improvement was 11.3 dB. With four interferers the improvement fell to 3.1 dB, and in 17 of 40 trials the array converged to a weight vector that reduced desired-signal gain by more than 2 dB. We attribute this to the array having only three degrees of freedom available for nulling once one is spent on the desired direction.\n\nConvergence took between 40 and 300 updates depending on the step size and the initial weight vector. A larger step size converged faster but oscillated in the two-interferer geometry.\n\nLimitations. All trials were run in an anechoic chamber. Multipath in a real building would present the array with many more apparent sources than interferers, and we expect the four-interferer degradation to appear at lower interferer counts outdoors. The array was not tested with moving interferers.\n\nConclusion. A four-element LMS array is sufficient for links with one or two fixed interferers and insufficient beyond that. Future work should test a six- or eight-element array under multipath and with moving sources."}
{"id":"synthetic-soil-2021","title":"Cover cropping and soil organic carbon on smallholder maize plots","year":2021,"scholarName":"Test Scholar","audience":"students","origin":"harvested","synthetic":true,"text":"Abstract. We measured whether a winter cover crop of hairy vetch changes soil organic carbon on smallholder maize plots over three seasons. Forty plots on two farms were split evenly between a cover-cropped treatment and a bare-fallow control.\n\nMethod. Each plot was 20 by 20 metres. Vetch was sown after the maize harvest in October and incorporated by hand in March before planting. Soil was sampled to 20 centimetres depth at the start of the study and after each maize harvest, five cores per plot, and carbon was measured by dry combustion. Maize yield was recorded per plot at each harvest.\n\nResults. After three seasons, cover-cropped plots held 0.34 percentage points more soil organic carbon than control plots, from a starting mean of 1.21 percent. The difference was not detectable after the first season and became detectable after the second. Maize yield did not differ between treatments in the first two seasons. In the third season cover-cropped plots yielded 8 percent more grain, and the difference was concentrated on the drier of the two farms.\n\nCosts. Vetch seed and the labour to incorporate it cost the equivalent of 6 percent of the maize revenue per plot per season. No farmer in the study abandoned the treatment, but four asked whether the incorporation could be done by animal traction instead of by hand.\n\nLimitations. Two farms in one district cannot speak for a region, and three seasons is short for a soil carbon response. The yield gain in the third season coincided with a dry year, and it is not possible from this design to separate the effect of the cover crop from the effect of the weather on the control plots.\n\nConclusion. On these plots a vetch cover crop raised soil organic carbon slowly and raised yield only in a dry season. Longer studies on more farms, and a trial of animal-drawn incorporation, are the next steps."}
{"id":"synthetic-reading-2019","title":"Sentence verification tasks as a measure of comprehension in grade seven readers","year":2019,"scholarName":"Test Scholar","audience":"general","origin":"harvested","synthetic":true,"text":"Abstract. We asked whether a short sentence verification task predicts reading comprehension in grade seven students as well as a standard multiple-choice test does, at a fraction of the testing time.\n\nMethod. 212 students in six classrooms read four expository passages of about 300 words each. After each passage, students judged twelve sentences as consistent or inconsistent with the passage. Half of the sentences preserved the passage's meaning in new words; half changed one relationship, such as a cause or a comparison. The same students took a 40-item multiple-choice comprehension test one week later. Teachers rated each student's reading on a five-point scale at the end of the term.\n\nResults. Scores on the verification task correlated with the multiple-choice test at 0.71 and with teacher ratings at 0.58. The verification task took a median of 14 minutes; the multiple-choice test took 45. Students found the meaning-changed sentences harder than the paraphrases: accuracy was 84 percent on paraphrases and 66 percent on changed sentences. Errors on changed sentences were concentrated on items that altered a causal relationship, where accuracy fell to 57 percent.\n\nLimitations. All passages were expository, and the sample came from one district. The correlation with the multiple-choice test may be inflated by the two tests sharing passages of similar difficulty. The teacher ratings were collected after the tests and could have been influenced by them.\n\nConclusion. A fourteen-minute sentence verification task tracks a forty-five-minute comprehension test closely enough to be useful for screening, and its error pattern points at causal relationships as the place where grade seven readers most often lose the meaning of a passage. Whether teaching aimed at that pattern raises comprehension is a separate question that this study does not answer."}
